  "query_preview": "..\n(Friday through Tuesday)\nIssued at 1232 AM CDT Wed Jul 22 2026\n\nFriday continues to be interesting weather day for the Panhandles\nas many of the models have seen the present upper-level high \npressure system retreat westward and force a boundary and moisture\ninto the area the prior day. This particular boundary is the \nfocal point of interest as quite a few models have placed it \nstalling in the Southern Texas Panhandles for Friday afternoon. \nThis stalled boundary could aid in a new round of showers and \nthunderstorms for the area, especially if we get a little extra \nmoisture push from what remain of Tropical Storm Bertha. Still \nthere is quite a bit uncertainty on where this boundary will \nactually stall, so these chances have only been around a 15 to 30 \npercent. Of course these chances could also see temperature \nfluctuate over the next 24 to 48 hours, though present models \nstill seem to favor most of the area reaching the triple digit \nmark yet again. \n\nMoving into the weekend and next week, heat continues to be the \nbig talking point as model agreement does not see the the upper-\nlevel high pressure staying over the Central and Western Untied \nStates laying off anytime soon. With this high-pressure in place, \nthe Panhandles will hold onto a more stagnate and dry upper-level \npattern that will aid in forcing temperatures near or over the \ntriple digit mark. Obviously such hot temperatures over multiple \nconsecutive days brings increasing confidence of some form of heat\nrelated products being needed across the Panhandles. However, \nwhat may not be as obvious is that low temperatures are not \nfairing much better with present expectations seeing locations, \nsuch as Amarillo, have the potential to either break or tie their \nrecord warm low temperature clear through Sunday. Expectations \nlike these would also mean that there is very little relief \noccurring during overnight hours which may only amplify the risk \nof seeing heat related illness as each day passes. \n\nIn short, the closing days of this week and possibly next week \nmay see a much higher risk for heat related illness not just from \nvery hot afternoon temperatures, but from the accumulating effect \nof seeing these hot temperatures for multiple day on top of \ngetting very little relief from the heat overnight.",
